{"A":"Explore ping-pong buffers in the OpenMP candidate from C: initialize both buffers identically, including untouched boundaries, then swap only after a complete stencil sweep. Preserve the convergence check and final-sweep behavior. The standalone swap candidates measured about 0.0584–0.0585 s, slower than C’s 0.0455 s, so test the combination rather than assuming a gain.","B":"Run a distinct experiment on convergence detection: compare the current per-cell/bitwise-OR flag approach with a per-row or per-thread maximum absolute change, using the same stencil values and threshold. Preserve the stopping rule and output. The previous SIMD/row-flag candidate measured 0.0675 s, so treat vectorization as a hypothesis and retain it only if measurements improve.","C":"Starting from the best measured candidate (11ce495741b04b02), investigate keeping one OpenMP team alive across Jacobi iterations to reduce repeated team-startup overhead. Synchronize the per-iteration convergence decision and preserve barriers between updates and any buffer swap. Measure the full candidate; do not assume this beats the existing per-iteration parallel region.","G":"Use C’s OpenMP candidate (0.0455 s; correct on 4 cases) as the incumbent against the 0.0667 s baseline. Integrate A’s buffer swap as a separate measured combination, ensuring both buffers have identical boundaries and the final completed sweep is the output. Evaluate B’s reduction variant separately; do not merge all changes by default. Run correctness and timing on each integrated candidate, and retain only combinations that are correct and actually faster. The contiguous-storage candidate measured 0.0615 s, so do not assume it helps either.","analysis":"The measured best is C’s OpenMP worksharing candidate at 0.04546 s versus the 0.06667 s baseline, on a 4-core ARM64 target. Standalone buffer swapping was about 0.0584–0.0585 s; contiguous storage was 0.06148 s; the SIMD/row-flag version was 0.06745 s. These results support using C as the incumbent, while treating further optimizations and combinations as experiments. Preserve Jacobi iteration, convergence and output semantics for every valid input."}